Requirements for the Requirements Report
Map My Mint is a budgeting app with all users in mind. The primary goal is to provide a locally kept program that offers a well-organized environment where users can manage their income, expenses, and goals all in a single place. Paired with a visually appealing map of these categories, the user is able to see a big picture of their money being spent, earned, and saved. Using a workflow of an Import-Map-Analyze cycle, data from all different types can be imported into the program, allowing for flexibility, time saving, and efficient budgeting for all users. 

Map by Mint provides users with several core features designed to help them manage their finances effectively.
-Local encrypted financial database
-Importing financial data from CSV or spreadsheet files
-Manual transaction entry
-Category-based spending organization
-Visual financial map dashboard 
-Real-time balance updates
-Savings goal creation and tracking
-Category spending limits
Workflows
Creation/Import
When a user begins using the program, the user will be prompted to create a local encrypted database file on their device. This ensures that data is kept secure and confidential. After the database is created, the user is then prompted to import or add their existing data into the database. This can be entered manually or via an imported spreadsheet, but all information is imported to a locally kept csv. The system will then go through the csv file and “map” the different categories that were chosen during import or that are in the csv imported file. It will then suggest file folders and subfolders to start organizing spending into.  The ability to add other folders is possible as well. After all the data has been imported and sorted, a visual map of his budget will appear, along with a total amount and percentage of his total income that is spent/budgeted for each category. But using a flow of secure initialization, data intake, category selection, categorization, and finishing with the visualization, this workflow is how a user would create and start using the program.
 
Short Term Audit
After a month or so of use and consistent data entry of their spending, the user decides to review their budget. They first start with a bird's-eye view of his budget. Bills, debt, and utilities all look like they should. Way to go!  However, the user gets to his spending and expenses and notices that the eating out category has really taken over that category, leaving almost no space for his grocery or gas categories. The user dives a little deeper into his eating out region and sees that a few of the gas station stops were accidentally miscategorized when added and should fall under the gas category. So he moves those. However, his stops at McDonald's, Culver's, and MOD Pizza have very obviously taken over that region of their budget. Knowing that they have to make some changes for the sake of their wallet and pants, the user puts a strict limit on the eating out folder. Now, when the user does eat out, they see the specific amount of “space” money that they have left for the month to eat out with. Allowing them to be conscious of his choices and really getting to dictate where his money is actually going. 

Goal Planning and Saving
After several months of progress on the user's budget, they set their sights on starting some goal planning. They see how much money they have saved over the past few months after taking their eating out budget down so that they have a surplus of money at the end of every month. The user is prompted to create a new goal folder. They then input the name of the goal, the target amount, and add a timeline. Breaking it up into as little as a week-by-week or month-by-month basis. Knowing that their budget is secure, the user can create sensitive goals such as an emergency fund, a down payment on a house, or even that cruise they have talked about. Once the goal is created, the user can track it visually within the map. This visual mapping transforms the surplus of money into a tangible asset, showing the user exactly how their disciplined spending in other categories is fulfilling their long term goals. 

