Non-Functional Requirements and Other Constraints
A description of non-functional (or non-behavioral) requirements and other constraints involved in the development of the system.  See slide 6 from the Chapter 4 notes.
Security:
The app will maintain security by protecting user login credentials and encrypting sensitive financial information. 
Additionally, all user input will be validated before being processed by the database, helping prevent injection and cross-site scripting attacks.
Availability:
All data will be stored locally, meaning that no internet connection is required for use. This allows users to access their accounts whenever they may need. 
Usability
The user interface will be intuitive to accommodate users with varying levels of technical experience. 
Financial data will be presented clearly through visualization, and navigation should require minimal steps to access key information.
Maintainability:
The system will be designed in a way that allows developers to update the application and add new features without too much difficulty/cost. 
It should also support modifications such as new budgeting tools, visualization features, or other financial reports.
Resilience:
If the system encounters an error or fails, it will continue to operate, and data should remain protected and recoverable in the event of unexpected system failure or attacks.
Responsiveness:
All results and displayed financial information will be given to the user within a reasonable time to keep the user engaged.
Actions like viewing the dashboard, importing financial data, and generating reports will occur quickly, so users can use and view without too much delay.
Reliability:
Our system features and behavior will be consistent with what is expected from both the developers and users.
Transaction tracking, budgeting calculations, and reports will produce accurate and consistent reports each time they're used.
Information provided back will be consistent with the current situations as well.

